\title{The Asymmetric/Symmetric Divisibility Dichotomy\\[4pt]
\large Residue invisibility of the $p-1$ weakness in semiprimes,\\
with an exact information-theoretic rate}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{14 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $N = pq$ be a semiprime. The Pollard $p-1$ and elliptic-curve factoring methods
succeed quickly precisely when a hidden factor $p$ has $p-1$ smooth. We ask whether
membership in this weak instance class is detectable from $N$ alone --- a
\emph{self-hint} --- and answer in the negative, in every form, identifying the exact
structural reason.

The reason is a dichotomy between one-sided and two-sided conditions on a
factorisation. In an arbitrary finite group $G$ (arithmetically $G=(\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$)
we prove that for every $A\subseteq G$ and every $n\in G$ the number of ordered
factorisations $n=ab$ with $a\in A$ equals $|A|$, independent of $n$, whereas the number
with $a\in A$ \emph{or} $b\in A$ equals $|A\cup nA^{-1}| = 2|A|-|A\cap nA^{-1}|$, which
genuinely varies. Informationally, the asymmetric mutual information is identically zero
for every finite group and every target, while the symmetric one is strictly positive for
every nontrivial target.

For the arithmetic target $A=\{1\}$ we compute the symmetric leak in closed form: in a
group of order $d$ it equals an explicit $I(d)$, which at $\ell=3$ is exactly
$\tfrac32-\tfrac34\log_2 3\in(0.30,0.32)$ bits, at $\ell=5$ lies in $(0.0355,0.036)$,
satisfies $I(d)<2/d^2$, and obeys the sharp asymptotic
$d^2I(d)\to \log_2 e-1=0.442695\ldots$, so the leak is asymptotically
$(\log_2 e-1)/(\ell-1)^2$ bits. These predictions reproduce the measured leaks
$0.313/0.036/0.015/0.005$ at $\ell=3,5,7,11$ to three decimals.

We classify the invisible targets (the leak vanishes exactly for perfect difference sets,
hence never nontrivially in abelian groups; proper subgroups are the \emph{most} visible
targets), separate information from advantage (for $\ell\ge 5$ no function of
$N\bmod\ell$ predicts the symmetric event better than a constant guess, and the threshold
is sharp), and prove unconditional impossibility results: for every $\ell>2$ and every
modulus $M$ no function of $N\bmod M$ decides $\ell\mid p-1$, the same for three-factor
moduli, no function of $N\bmod 1155$ decides $10$-smoothness of $p-1$, and the public
$N\pm1$ smoothness bits are logically independent of the secret bit. A tropicalisation
shows the dichotomy is a property of the fibration $G\times G\to G$ valued in an arbitrary
commutative monoid.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Weak instances and the self-hint question}

The security of RSA-type systems rests on the difficulty of recovering $p$ and $q$ from
$N=pq$, a statement about the \emph{typical} instance. Certain instances are far easier.
The oldest such family is the one exploited by Pollard's $p-1$ method and, more robustly,
by the elliptic curve method: if a factor $p$ has $p-1$ $B$-smooth --- every prime divisor
of $p-1$ is at most $B$ --- then $p$ is recoverable from $N$ in time roughly
$\tilde O(B)$ by computing $\gcd(a^{k}-1,N)$ for a highly composite exponent $k$.

Empirically this weakness is common at moderate sizes: among random $k$-bit semiprimes
with $k\in\{14,16,18\}$ (up to $2\times 10^5$ samples per size), between $60\%$ and $78\%$
have a factor $p$ with $p-1$ smooth to the bound $B=1000$. The base rate agrees with an
even-adjusted Dickman estimate $\rho_{\mathrm{even}}(\log(2^k/2)/\log B)$ to within about
$0.04$, the residual being a powers-of-two effect ($p-1$ is always even and its $2$-adic
valuation is biased).

If a large fraction of keys are weak and weakness were detectable from the public modulus,
an adversary could sort a corpus of keys and attack only the profitable ones. We call any
statistic computable from $N$ and correlated with the secret weakness a \emph{self-hint}.
Does one exist?

\subsection{Summary of the answer}

No. The proof descends to the atom of smoothness: for a small prime $\ell$, the divisibility
event $\ell\mid p-1$, i.e.\ $p\equiv 1 \pmod \ell$. Two versions of the question behave
completely differently:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{asymmetric:} $\ell\mid p-1$ (a designated factor);
\item \textbf{symmetric:} $\ell\mid p-1$ or $\ell\mid q-1$ (the unordered pair).
\end{itemize}
Measured against $N\bmod\ell$, the mutual informations are $0.0000$--$0.0005$ bits for the
asymmetric event at every $\ell\in\{3,5,7,11\}$ and every key size, versus
$0.313/0.036/0.015/0.005$ bits for the symmetric one. We prove the first column is exactly
zero and compute the second in closed form.

\section{The model}

Fix an odd prime $\ell$ and consider semiprimes $N=pq$ with $p,q\ne\ell$. Reduction modulo
$\ell$ sends $p,q$ to elements $a,b$ of $G=(\mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z})^\times$,
$|G|=\ell-1$, and sends $N$ to $n=ab$. The event $\ell\mid p-1$ becomes $a\in A$ for the
singleton target $A=\{1\}$.

Residues of random primes equidistribute over $G$, so we adopt the \emph{uniform ordered
pair model}: draw $(a,b)$ uniformly from $G\times G$ and set $n=ab$; the observable is $n$
and the secret is a Boolean event determined by $(a,b)$. All statements below hold for an
arbitrary finite group $G$ and arbitrary $A\subseteq G$.

\begin{definition}[Fibres]
For $A\subseteq G$ and $n\in G$ put
\[
\Fasym_A(n)=\{(a,b): ab=n,\ a\in A\},\qquad
\Fsym_A(n)=\{(a,b): ab=n,\ a\in A \text{ or } b\in A\}.
\]
\end{definition}

The fibre of the multiplication map over any $n$ has exactly $|G|$ elements, since $a$
determines $b=a^{-1}n$. Hence the conditional probability of the asymmetric (resp.\
symmetric) event given $n$ is $|\Fasym_A(n)|/|G|$ (resp.\ $|\Fsym_A(n)|/|G|$), and
everything reduces to counting fibres.

\section{The counting dichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[Asymmetric invisibility]\label{thm:asym}
For every finite group $G$, every $A\subseteq G$ and every $n\in G$,
$\bigl|\Fasym_A(n)\bigr| = |A|$; in particular the count does not depend on $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The map $(a,b)\mapsto a$ sends $\Fasym_A(n)$ into $A$; it is injective because $ab=ab'$
forces $b=b'$; and it is surjective because $(a,a^{-1}n)$ lies in the fibre for every
$a\in A$.
\end{proof}

Thus in the fibration $\mu:G\times G\to G$, $\mu(a,b)=ab$, the first-coordinate projection
restricted to any fibre is a bijection onto $G$: a one-sided condition pulls back to a set
of constant size. The product knows nothing about a designated factor.

\begin{theorem}[Symmetric count]\label{thm:sym}
For every finite group $G$, every $A\subseteq G$ and every $n\in G$,
\[
\bigl|\Fsym_A(n)\bigr| = \bigl|A\cup nA^{-1}\bigr| = 2|A| - \bigl|A\cap nA^{-1}\bigr|,
\qquad nA^{-1}=\{na^{-1}:a\in A\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Project to the first coordinate as before. The pair $(a,a^{-1}n)$ satisfies the symmetric
condition iff $a\in A$ or $a^{-1}n\in A$, i.e.\ $a\in nA^{-1}$; so the image is
$A\cup nA^{-1}$. Since $b\mapsto nb^{-1}$ is injective, $|nA^{-1}|=|A|$, and
inclusion--exclusion gives the second form.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Autocorrelation]
$\alpha_A(n):=|A\cap nA^{-1}|$.
\end{definition}

The symmetric count is $2|A|-\alpha_A(n)$: constant exactly when the autocorrelation is.

\begin{corollary}[The singleton case]\label{cor:singleton}
For $A=\{1\}$ one has $|\Fasym_{\{1\}}(n)|=1$ for all $n$, while
\[
|\Fsym_{\{1\}}(n)| = \begin{cases} 1, & n=1,\\ 2, & n\ne 1.\end{cases}
\]
\end{corollary}

Dividing by $|G|=\ell-1$:
\[
\Pr[\ell\mid p-1 \mid N\equiv n] = \frac{1}{\ell-1}\ \text{for every }n,
\qquad
\Pr[\ell\mid p-1 \text{ or } \ell\mid q-1 \mid N\equiv n] \in \Bigl\{\frac{1}{\ell-1},\frac{2}{\ell-1}\Bigr\},
\]
the latter equal to $1/(\ell-1)$ iff $n=1$. At $\ell=3$ the second value is $1$: certainty.

\subsection{The exact $\ell=3$ mechanism}

\begin{theorem}[Forcing modulo $3$]\label{thm:force3}
Let $p,q$ be primes with $p\ne 3\ne q$ and $N=pq\equiv 2\pmod 3$. Then $3\mid p-1$ or
$3\mid q-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Neither factor is divisible by $3$, so each is $\equiv 1$ or $2 \pmod 3$. If both were
$\equiv 2$ then $N\equiv 4\equiv 1$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

The complementary class is genuinely ambiguous: $55=5\cdot11\equiv 1$ has neither factor
$\equiv1$, while $91=7\cdot13\equiv1$ has both.

\begin{theorem}[No residue dial at $\ell=3$]
There is no function $f$ with $(3\mid p-1)\iff f(N\bmod 3)$ for all semiprimes $N=pq$,
$p<q$, $p,q\ne 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$77=7\cdot11$ and $65=5\cdot13$ are both $\equiv 2\pmod 3$, yet $3\mid 7-1$ and
$3\nmid 5-1$.
\end{proof}

Both residue classes carry both outcomes ($77,65$ in class $2$; $91,55$ in class $1$), so
the failure is per-class, not merely on average.

\begin{remark}
The $\ell=3$ forcing is exceptional. At $\ell=5$ no class forces the symmetric event:
$91\equiv1$, $247=13\cdot19\equiv2$, $133=7\cdot19\equiv3$, $119=7\cdot17\equiv4 \pmod 5$,
and none has a factor $\equiv1\pmod 5$. Forcing at $\ell=3$ occurs because a two-element
fibre is exhausted by a two-element event.
\end{remark}

\section{Information}

\begin{definition}
For a joint mass function $P$ on $X\times Y$ ($X,Y$ finite), the mutual information in bits
is
\[
I(P)=\sum_{x}\sum_{y}P(x,y)\log_2\frac{P(x,y)}{P_X(x)P_Y(y)},\qquad
P_X(x)=\sum_y P(x,y),\ P_Y(y)=\sum_x P(x,y),
\]
with the convention $0\log 0=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Product laws]\label{prop:prod}
If $P(x,y)=r(x)c(y)$ with $\sum r=\sum c=1$, then $I(P)=0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Gibbs]\label{prop:gibbs}
$I(P)\ge 0$ always, with strict inequality as soon as
$P(x_0,y_0)\ne P_X(x_0)P_Y(y_0)$ for some cell.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pointwise $-P\log\frac{P}{rc}\le rc-P$ from $\log t\le t-1$; summing, the right-hand side
telescopes to $1-1=0$, giving $I\ge0$. An off-product cell makes its inequality strict.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero asymmetric leak]\label{thm:zeroleak}
For every finite group $G$ and every $A\subseteq G$, the joint law of the secret bit
``$a\in A$'' and the observable $n=ab$ in the uniform ordered pair model has mutual
information exactly $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:asym} the two fibre counts are $|A|$ and $|G|-|A|$ for every $n$, so
the joint law is
\[
P(\varepsilon,n) = \frac{|A_\varepsilon|}{|G|}\cdot\frac{1}{|G|},
\qquad A_{\text{true}}=A,\ A_{\text{false}}=G\setminus A,
\]
a product law; apply Proposition~\ref{prop:prod}.
\end{proof}

This is the theoretical counterpart of the measured $0.0000$--$0.0005$ bits: the value is
structurally zero, for \emph{any} property of the designated factor.

\subsection{The $\ell=3$ tables}

With $G=\{1,-1\}$, rows indexed by $n$ and columns by (event true, event false),
\[
P^{\mathrm{asym}}=\begin{pmatrix}1/4&1/4\\1/4&1/4\end{pmatrix},\qquad
P^{\mathrm{sym}}=\begin{pmatrix}1/4&1/4\\1/2&0\end{pmatrix},
\]
the corner zero being Theorem~\ref{thm:force3}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:mi3}
$I(P^{\mathrm{asym}})=0$ and
$I(P^{\mathrm{sym}})=\tfrac32-\tfrac34\log_2 3 = 0.311278\ldots\in(0.30,0.32)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first table is a product of uniform marginals. For the second the row marginals are
$(1/2,1/2)$ and the column marginals $(3/4,1/4)$; expanding four terms with
$\log_2(2/3)=1-\log_2 3$ and $\log_2(4/3)=2-\log_2 3$ gives the closed value. The bracket
follows from $19/12<\log_2 3<27/17$, i.e.\ $2^{19}=524288<531441=3^{12}$ and
$3^{17}=129140163<134217728=2^{27}$.
\end{proof}

The measurement was $0.313$ bits.

\begin{theorem}[Information dichotomy]
For every odd prime $\ell$ the symmetric joint law has strictly positive mutual
information, while the asymmetric one has mutual information exactly $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:singleton} the symmetric counts take two values, so the joint law is
off-product at $n=1$ and Proposition~\ref{prop:gibbs} applies;
Theorem~\ref{thm:zeroleak} gives the other half.
\end{proof}

\section{Which targets are invisible?}

\begin{theorem}[Classification by fibre constancy]\label{thm:class}
The symmetric leak vanishes if and only if $|\Fsym_A(n)|$ is independent of $n$;
equivalently, by Theorem~\ref{thm:sym}, iff the autocorrelation
$\alpha_A(n)=|A\cap nA^{-1}|$ is constant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Constant count $k$ makes the joint law $(\text{uniform in } n)\times(\text{Bernoulli }k/|G|)$,
so $I=0$; two distinct counts make it off-product in the corresponding cell, so $I>0$.
\end{proof}

Constant autocorrelation is the defining property of a \emph{perfect difference set}:
invisibility is a rare design condition, not a default.

\begin{theorem}[Subgroups always leak]
Let $H\le G$ be a proper subgroup. Then $|\Fsym_H(n)|=|H|$ for $n\in H$ and $2|H|$
otherwise, so the leak is strictly positive.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$H\cup nH^{-1}=H\cup nH$, which is $H$ when $n\in H$ and a disjoint union of two cosets
otherwise; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:class}.
\end{proof}

This refutes the guess that cosets of subgroups are invisible: they are the \emph{most}
visible targets. Arithmetic instances: ``$\ell\mid x-1$'' ($H$ trivial), ``$x$ is a $k$-th
power mod $\ell$'', ``$x$ is a quadratic residue mod $\ell$''.

\begin{theorem}[Abelian classification]\label{thm:abel}
Let $G$ be a finite commutative group and $A\subseteq G$. Then $\alpha_A$ is constant iff
$A=\emptyset$ or $A=G$. Consequently the symmetric leak is strictly positive for every
nontrivial $A$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $r_A(n)=\#\{(a,b)\in A\times A: ab=n\}$ be the representation function; a change of
variables gives $\alpha_A=r_A$. For a character $\psi$ put $S(\psi)=\sum_{a\in A}\psi(a)$;
then $S(\psi)^2=\sum_n r_A(n)\psi(n)$. If $r_A\equiv c$ the right-hand side is
$c\sum_n\psi(n)=0$ for every nontrivial $\psi$, so $S(\psi)=0$ there; Fourier inversion of
$\mathbf 1_A$ then gives $|G|\mathbf 1_A(g)=|A|$ for all $g$, forcing $|A|\in\{0,|G|\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
In a finite commutative group, for every target the asymmetric leak is $0$, and for every
nontrivial target the symmetric leak is $>0$. Since $(\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$ is cyclic,
no nontrivial divisibility-type event on the factors is symmetrically invisible.
\end{corollary}

\section{The exact size of the leak, and its rate}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
Let $|G|=d\ge2$ and $A=\{1\}$. The symmetric mutual information equals
\[
I(d)=\frac{1}{d^2}\left[
\log_2\frac{d}{2d-1}
+(d-1)\log_2\frac{d}{d-1}
+2(d-1)\log_2\frac{2d}{2d-1}
+(d-1)(d-2)\log_2\frac{d(d-2)}{(d-1)^2}
\right].
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Corollary~\ref{cor:singleton} the joint law has cells
$(1/d^2,(d-1)/d^2)$ over $n=1$ and $(2/d^2,(d-2)/d^2)$ over each of the $d-1$ other
values, with row marginals $1/d$ and column marginals $(2d-1)/d^2$ and $(d-1)^2/d^2$.
Substituting and collecting the four cell types yields the four bracketed terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every odd prime $\ell$, the mutual information between $N\bmod\ell$ and the symmetric
divisibility event is exactly $I(\ell-1)$.
\end{corollary}

